= Spotlight effect — Simple =

<onlyinclude>The '''spotlight effect''' is a tendency studied in social psychology in which people overestimate how much other people notice their appearance or actions. For example, someone wearing an unusual shirt may expect strangers to notice it more than they actually do. The finding concerns estimates in specific social situations; it does not mean that nobody notices anyone, or that every person overestimates attention in every setting.<ref name="gilovich">Thomas Gilovich, Victoria H. Medvec and Kenneth Savitsky, [https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.78.2.211 “The spotlight effect in social judgment”], ''Journal of Personality and Social Psychology'' 78(2), 211–222 (2000).</ref></onlyinclude>

== An everyday example ==

Imagine spilling a little coffee on your shirt just before a meeting. Because you know exactly where the stain is, you may think everybody sees it. But many people in the room are thinking about other things and may not notice at all.

This example illustrates the idea; it does not establish how often a real stain is noticed.

== Why might we overestimate attention? ==

We have immediate access to our own appearance, feelings and actions. It may be difficult to adjust our estimate of what other people can see or have time to observe.

Gilovich, Medvec and Savitsky investigated this kind of judgment through controlled studies, comparing participants' estimates of how noticeable they were with other people's actual reports.<ref name="gilovich"/>

One explanation involves anchoring on one's own experience and adjusting insufficiently for the observer's different perspective. The explanation is a hypothesis supported in specific experiments, not a diagnosis of every instance of self-consciousness.

== Does this mean nobody cares? ==

No. In some contexts people really are observed closely, such as public performances, safety-critical duties or formal evaluations. Noticeability also varies with how striking an event is, whether the observer knows what to look for and whether observers are actively watching.

The claim is about inaccurate estimates in suitable conditions, not a universal rule about attention.

== How is it different from social anxiety? ==

The spotlight effect describes a judgmental bias that can occur without a clinical condition. Social anxiety involves a much broader set of experiences and is not the same definition. One should not infer someone's psychological health from a single overestimate of being noticed.

It is also different from the '''illusion of transparency''', in which people overestimate how easily others can detect their private thoughts or emotions. These related judgments concern different observables.<ref name="review">Thomas Gilovich and Kenneth Savitsky, [https://doi.org/10.1111/1467-8721.00039 “The Spotlight Effect and the Illusion of Transparency”], ''Current Directions in Psychological Science'' 8(6) (1999).</ref>

== What do experiments measure? ==

In a simple research design, a participant estimates how many observers will remember an unusual shirt. The observers are then asked whether they actually noticed or can identify it.

The difference between prediction and observation is the quantity of interest. Researchers also need enough observations, clear methods and independent replication.

== A practical use ==

If you suspect that everyone noticed a small mistake, you can consider other explanations and look for evidence rather than treating your immediate impression as certainty. The principle does not justify ignoring real feedback or social consequences.

[[Cognition]], [[Metacognition]] and [[Reasoning]] provide related research concepts.

